# AryaOS dronecot (DroneScout DS101/DS110) config — /etc/default/dronecot-dronescout
#
# A BlueMark DroneScout DS101 / DroneBeacon emits detected Remote ID as MAVLink
# over its USB-serial. dronecot's SerialWorker reads it (OPEN_DRONE_ID_MESSAGE_PACK
# and, as of dronecot 2.2.3, ADSB_VEHICLE) and forwards to charontak as CoT.
# COT_URL is inherited from aryaos-config.txt via the service EnvironmentFile.
#
# Enable on a box with a DS101:  sudo systemctl enable --now dronecot-dronescout
#
# PORT: the DS101 is an ESP32-S3 (udev "Espressif USB JTAG/serial debug unit",
# 303a:1001). Its /dev/serial/by-id path embeds the per-unit MAC, so AryaOS pins
# a stable /dev/dronescout symlink via 99-aryaos-dronescout.rules — that is what
# FEED_URL points at below. Do not put the raw Espressif by-id path here: its MAC
# contains colons, and pymavlink mistakes any colon-bearing device name for a UDP
# endpoint. If the box has multiple ESP32-S3 devices (e.g. a DS101 and SiKW00F),
# give the selected serial a unique, colon-free udev symlink and use that path.
# Live-verified 2026-08-10 against a DroneScout receiving
# OPEN_DRONE_ID_MESSAGE_PACK at 115200 baud on the ESP32-S3 CDC port.
# A DS110 connected through a USB-UART adapter is written here automatically by
# `aryaos-role discover --apply`, but only after checksum-valid Remote-ID MAVLink
# is observed. The adapter's generic USB identity is not sufficient evidence.

FEED_URL=serial:///dev/dronescout:115200
SENSOR_ID=dronescout
